Wait — careful with `decideGitStageRoute` vs the original inline logic. Original:

```js
if (gitResult.success && !gitResult.noNewCommit) { ... create PR ... }
else if (gitResult.noNewCommit) { noCodeChanges = true; ... }
else { return resetToBacklogAfterGitFailure(...); }
``

Subtle: when `gitResult.success` is falsy but `gitResult.noNewCommit` is truthy — original: first cond false (success false), second `else if (gitResult.noNewCommit)` TRUE → noCodeChanges = true! My helper: `gitResult && gitResult.success && gitResult.noNewCommit` → false → 'git-failure'. DIFFERENT.

Hmm wait — but when is `performGitOperations` returning `success:false`? It returns `{ success: false, error: ... }` — noNewCommit never set on failure. So in practice success:false implies noNewCommit undefined. But "behaviour unchanged" is the rule — mirror EXACTLY:

- route 'create-pr' iff `gitResult.success && !gitResult.noNewCommit`
- else 'no-new-commit' iff `gitResult.noNewCommit` (regardless of success!)
- else 'git-failure'

Let me rewrite:

```js
function decideGitStageRoute(gitResult) {
   if (gitResult && gitResult.success && !gitResult.noNewCommit) return 'create-pr';
   if (gitResult && gitResult.noNewCommit) return 'no-new-commit';
   return 'git-failure';
}
``

Original second condition is just `gitResult.noNewCommit` on the else-branch of the first — so a falsy gitResult: `gitResult.noNewCommit` would throw on null. In the original code gitResult is always an object (performGitOperations always returns an object). If gitResult were null the original would throw at `gitResult.success` → caught by outer catch → error comment + failure return. My helper returning 'git-failure' would instead produce resetToBacklogAfterGitFailure — DIFFERENT behavior for a null gitResult.

To be exactly faithful: keep the helper evaluating the same expressions without null-guarding:

```js
function decideGitStageRoute(gitResult) {
   if (gitResult.success && !gitResult.noNewCommit) return 'create-pr';
   if (gitResult.noNewCommit) return 'no-new-commit';
   return 'git-failure';
